@startuml flow-account-recovery
' Recovering an account with a one-time code, and why it does not end at "signed in".
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-account-recovery.puml

' Explicit near-white, NOT transparent: the text is dark, and a transparent background
' renders black on a dark viewer, which makes the whole diagram unreadable.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #59606F
  LifeLineBorderColor #C3CAD8
  ParticipantBorderColor #5B5BD6
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FDFDFD
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #C2670A
}

title Account recovery by one-time code

actor User
participant "SPA\n/code" as SPA
participant AuthController as API
participant OtpService as OTP
database "otp_codes" as DB
participant BrevoMailClient as Mail
participant AuthService as Auth
participant JwtService as JWT

== 1. Ask for a code ==

User -> SPA : enters email
SPA -> API : POST /auth/otp/request
API -> OTP : requestCode(email)

OTP -> DB : count requests in the last hour

note right of OTP
  Rate limit is checked **before** the account
  lookup. Throttling only real accounts would
  make a 429 mean "this address is registered".
end note

alt over the hourly budget
  OTP --> API : 429
else address has no account
  OTP -> DB : record the request, issue no code
  note right of OTP : Counts towards the limit,\nbut holds no secret.
  OTP --> API : null
else registered
  OTP -> DB : supersede outstanding, store SHA-256 of a new code

  alt demo mode ON (development only)
    OTP --> API : the code itself
    note right of OTP #FFE0E0
      A separate branch, taken before delivery
      is attempted. Never a fallback.
    end note
  else demo mode OFF
    OTP -> Mail : send(code)
    alt provider accepted (HTTP 201)
      Mail --> OTP : true
      OTP --> API : null
    else anything else
      Mail --> OTP : false
      OTP -> DB : **delete the stored code**
      OTP --> API : 503
      note right of OTP #FFE0E0
        Nothing was sent, so nothing may be
        honoured — and the code is never
        returned to the caller.
      end note
    end
  end
end

API --> SPA : 202 { demoMode, demoCode }
note over SPA : Same wording whether or not the\naddress is registered.

== 2. Redeem it ==

User -> SPA : enters the six digits
SPA -> API : POST /auth/otp/verify
API -> Auth : loginWithCode(email, code)
Auth -> OTP : verifyForSignIn(email, code)
OTP -> DB : find by (email, hash), unexpired
OTP -> DB : destroy the hash — single use

note right of OTP
  The account is resolved from the address the
  code was **issued to**, stored on the row —
  never from anything the caller sent with it.
end note

Auth -> JWT : generateCodeVerifiedAccessToken(...)
JWT --> Auth : token carrying **vbc**
Auth --> SPA : TokenResponse

== 3. Set a password — presented, not offered ==

note over SPA
  Without this step the code becomes a permanent
  second credential: somebody who forgot their
  password signs in by code forever and never
  actually recovers the account.
end note

User -> SPA : new password
SPA -> API : POST /auth/change-password\n{ newPassword } — no proof sent
API -> Auth : changePassword(principal, request, bearerToken)

Auth -> JWT : isVerifiedByCode(bearerToken)
JWT --> Auth : true, if within 15 min of issue

Auth -> Auth : encode, save, revoke every refresh token
Auth --> SPA : { username, tokens }

note over SPA
  The **username** is shown. Recovery found the
  account by email, but sign-in wants a username
  — and for a Google-created account those differ.
  Without showing it, the next sign-in fails with
  "invalid username or password" and nothing on
  screen explains why.
end note

@enduml
